.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_MSG_COUNT 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_msg_count \- get the current message count of a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

size_t ccol_circq_msg_count(ccol_circular_queue *cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the number of messages that the queue holds at the moment of
the call. The count is read under the queue's lock, so it is a
consistent value and never a torn read.

It is still only a snapshot. Another thread can send or receive right
after the lock is released, so by the time you look at the value it
describes a moment that has passed. It is fine for reports and for a
drain loop, but to find out whether the queue has room or holds a
message, call ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(),
which check and act in one step that nothing can come between.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue to ask
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The number of messages in the queue, or ccol_invalid_size
((size_t)-1) when cq is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Zero is an ordinary result and means that the queue is empty. Only
ccol_invalid_size reports an error.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_chan_msg_count (3)
